Iterative Binary Search: Search algorithm that finds the position of a target value within a sorted array. 
Half of the array is eliminated during each "step"
  - Not efficient for small data sets; preferred for large ones
  - Array must be sorted
  - Uses a while loop
  - Uses two pointers: low and high
  - Updates these pointers until low > high

Example: https://medium.com/@maulikukreti07/binary-search-iterative-vs-recursive-7b8fc14d9315
int binarySearch(int arr[],int n,int key)
 {
   int low=0,high=n-1;
   while(low<=high)
   {
     int mid=low+(high-low)/2;
     if(arr[mid]==key)return mid;
     else if(arr[mid]<key)low=mid+1;
     else high=mid-1;
   }
 return -1;
}
  - Run time complexity: O(log n)
  - Space Complexity: O(1)

Recursive Binary Search: Function calls itself on a subarray until it hits the base case
  - Time complexity: O(log n)
  - Space complexity: O(log n)
  - Logic:
    - If low > high, the element is not there
    - Recursive step: Call the function again with updated boundaries
Example: 
int binarySearch(int arr[],int low,int high,int key) 
{
 if(low>high)return -1;
 int mid=low+(high-low) / 2;
 if(arr[mid] == key)return mid;
 else if(arr[mid]>key)
 return binarySearch(arr,low,mid-1,key);
 else
 return binarySearch(arr,mid+1,high,key);
}


Assignment: Implement binary search from scratch in C++17. Your program must work on a sorted vector and 
return the index of the target or -1 when the target is absent.
Required implementation: 

  - Implement an iterative binary search.
  - Implement a recursive binary search.
  - Instrument both versions to count element comparisons.
  - Test at least five searches, including: first element, last element, middle element,
    missing value below the range, and missing value inside the range.

  - For the recursive version, write and explain the recurrence T(n) = T(n/2) + O(1) and connect it 
    to O(log n).
      - T(n) = time needed to search n elements
      - T(n/2) after checking the middle, we only need to search about half the elements
      - O(1) = checking the middle element and deciding which half to search takes constant time
      - Summary: Each recursive call searches only half of the remaining vector, while finding the middle 
        element and comparing it to the target takes constant time. 

  - Compare binary search to a linear search on the same data.
  - Restriction: do not call std::binary_search, lower_bound, or another library search routine to 
    perform the required search
